{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"dowdy-antarctic-junco","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\n`rebar/host:review_interrupts` is published DIMENSIONLESS, but `autodeploy.sh` emits its\n`AUTODEPLOY_REVIEW_INTERRUPT` marker for two causes with a `reason` field that the metric\nthrows away:\n\n- `bound-exceeded`   — `DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX` was spent with reviews still in flight. The bot is\n                       busy; the drain check is WORKING.\n- `signal-unavailable` — `/health`'s `in_flight` was unreadable, so the deploy ran WITHOUT a\n                       drain check. The probe itself is broken and every deploy is blind.\n\nThese have OPPOSITE remediations, and the alarm cannot tell them apart.\n\n## Why this is worth its own ticket\n\nThis is the concrete cost, not a hypothetical. [rebar:7b4a-0f39-1a45-4ce9] was filed by an\nautomated CloudWatch sweep that could see the firings but not their reason, so it had to hedge\nbetween \"the bot is busy\" and \"34cd has silently regressed and every deploy is blind\" — and its\nown AC1 was \"read the reason from `journalctl` on the box\". Answering it required SSM shell\naccess to i-00880b2c7f13527c5. It turned out to be `bound-exceeded` on all four firings, but\nthat could not be known from the metric.\n\nThe alarm description in `monitoring_autodeploy.tf` already tells the investigator to go read\nthe journal for the reason field. Splitting the metric removes that hop, and matters most for\n`signal-unavailable`, which is the urgent one and is currently indistinguishable from routine\nburst residue.\n\n## Scope — observability only, but it needs an apply\n\nBehaviour-preserving for autodeploy and for reviews: nothing about deferral, the bound, or\nreview execution changes. It is confined to how the existing marker is counted and alarmed.\n\nLikely shape (not prescriptive): either distinct journal tokens counted into separate metrics\n(mirroring how `observability.sh` §4e already keeps `deploy_deferrals` separate from\n`review_interrupts`), or a `reason` dimension — noting the repo's standing convention that a\ndimension must be added on BOTH sides or CloudWatch silently unmatches (monitoring_s4b.tf).\nKeeping a rolled-up total so existing history stays readable is worth considering.\n\n**This is a Terraform alarm-contract change and therefore needs an operator `terraform apply`;\nit is not a code-only change that can land and take effect on merge.** Sequence it with\nwhatever disposition is reached on the deferral bound.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A future CloudWatch-only sweep can tell `bound-exceeded` from `signal-unavailable`\n      WITHOUT shell access to the host.\n- [ ] `signal-unavailable` remains promptly alarming (it is the \"deploys are running blind\"\n      case; today it latches on a single datapoint).\n- [ ] Metric and alarm agree on dimensions on both sides.\n- [ ] The Terraform change is applied, not merely merged, and that is recorded.","id":"613a-e3f4-14a6-45a3","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"split review_interrupts by reason so a CloudWatch-only sweep can tell bound-exceeded from signal-unavailable"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786518175196593001,"uuid":"90740f08-1952-4df6-896d-4489c3300545"}